+### UI explanation flags
+
+Vanilla cards that use a keyword mechanic show small **explainer boxes**
+alongside the description wherever the card's full tooltip appears — the
+shop, the collection, and hovering the card mid-run. Each box is a title
+plus vanilla's one-line definition of the mechanic, already translated into
+the game's eleven languages.
+
+Each `Show*` method turns exactly one box on for your card. All fourteen are
+off by default, and they are display-only — no gameplay effect.
+
+**Enable the flag for every keyword your description uses, and no others.**
+That is what vanilla cards do: the player who has never seen the mechanic
+gets vanilla's own definition of it in their language, and unrelated boxes
+just crowd the tooltip.
+
+Piece states:
+
+| Method | Explainer box | Vanilla's text | Description markup |
+| ------ | ------------- | -------------- | ------------------ |
+| `.ShowGolden()` | GOLDEN PIECE | "Give +$2 at the end of the game and reset to DEFAULT piece." | `<color=∞>` |
+| `.ShowBless()` | BLESSED PIECE | "Once captured, return to your stock." | `<color=ƒ>` |
+| `.ShowProtect()` | PROTECTED PIECE | "Can't be captured." | `<color=æ>` |
+| `.ShowTrap()` | TRAPPED PIECE | "Can't move." | `<color=◊>` |
+| `.ShowPhantom()` | PHANTOM PIECE | "Sell for $0 and disappear at the end of a game." | `<color=ß>` |
+
+Tiles — each explains the tile that applies the matching piece state:
+
+| Method | Explainer box | Vanilla's text | Description markup |
+| ------ | ------------- | -------------- | ------------------ |
+| `.ShowGoldenTile()` | GOLDEN TILE | "Moving a piece on this tile turns it into a GOLDEN PIECE." | `<sprite=1>` `<color=∞>` |
+| `.ShowBlessedTile()` | BLESS TILE | "Moving a piece on this tile BLESSES it." | `<sprite=0>` `<color=ƒ>` |
+| `.ShowProtectedTile()` | PROTECT TILE | "Moving a piece on this tile PROTECTS it." | `<sprite=2>` `<color=æ>` |
+| `.ShowTrapTile()` | TRAP TILE | "When an enemy piece steps on this tile, TRAPS it." | `<sprite=3>` `<color=◊>` |
+| `.ShowPhantomTile()` | PHANTOM TILE | "Moving a piece on this tile grants a PHANTOM copy of it." | `<sprite=12>` `<color=ß>` |
+
+Actions and concepts:
+
+| Method | Explainer box | Vanilla's text | Description markup |
+| ------ | ------------- | -------------- | ------------------ |
+| `.ShowLanding()` | LANDING | "Placing a piece from stock to board during a game." | `<color=©>` |
+| `.ShowWait()` | WAIT | "Skip your turn without playing." | `<color=µ>` |
+| `.ShowPromotion()` | PROMOTION | "Transform a PAWN into another piece when it reaches the end of the board." | `<rainb l=0.5>` |
+| `.ShowConsideredAs()` | COUNTED AS | "For Gambit effects only, this piece is treated as the specified piece. (This piece keeps its normal movement)." | — |
+
+The **Description markup** column is the matching vocabulary from
+[Writing card text](Your-First-Gambit#writing-card-text): a description that
+says `<color=◊>TRAP</color>` tiles is a card that sets `.ShowTrapTile()`.
+That is exactly what the SpikesGambit sample does — "Enemy pieces that step
+on TRAP tiles are captured instead of trapped" sets `.ShowTrapTile()` and
+nothing else. A card that referenced both trap tiles and landing would set
+both flags.
+
+One vanilla explainer has no flag: ADJACENT PIECE ("Refers to every piece
+next to another one vertically or horizontally."). GambitApi doesn't expose
+it — if your card cares about adjacency, spell the rule out in your
+description instead.
 
