 ## `Gambonanza.GambitApi.ModGambitApi`
 
+Static convenience surface. The builder is the usual way in; everything else
+here is for the cases the builder doesn't cover.
+
 ```csharp
-public static Sprite LoadSprite(string path);
+public static GambitBuilder CreateGambit(string id);
+public static void   Register(GambitDefinition def);
+public static void   RegisterAll(params GambitDefinition[] defs);
+public static void   Unlock(string gambitId);
+public static Sprite LoadSprite(string filePath, Vector2? pivot = null);
 ```
 
-Loads a PNG with the right pixel-art settings: `FilterMode.Point`, pivot
-`(0.5, 0.5)`, and a PPU that `Register()` later rebuilds from the cloned
+| Member | Notes |
+| ------ | ----- |
+| `CreateGambit(id)` | Alias for `GambitBuilder.Create(id)`. |
+| `Register(def)` | Registers a hand-built `GambitDefinition` — the plain-data object the builder fills. Its public fields map 1:1 to the builder methods (plus the `Show*` bools as plain fields). Rarity and focus are re-validated at registration, so a definition assembled without the builder gets the same guards. |
+| `RegisterAll(defs)` | `Register` in a loop. |
+| `Unlock(id)` | Force-unlock a gambit right now. Pair with `.AutoUnlock(false)` to gate your gambit behind your own condition. Unlike `AutoUnlock` — which queues and retries until the game's unlock manager is up — this is a one-shot: it silently does nothing if called before the unlock manager exists. |
+| `LoadSprite(path, pivot?)` | PNG loader with the right pixel-art settings — details below. |
+
+### `LoadSprite`
+
+Loads a PNG with `FilterMode.Point` and an optional pivot (default centre,
+`(0.5, 0.5)`). Any pixel size works: the PPU you get here is provisional, and
+`Register()` rebuilds the in-game sprite with a PPU computed from the cloned
 template's world height, so your card lands at vanilla's on-board size at any
 resolution.
 
 Returns `null` if the file is missing — the builder then falls back to a
-placeholder, which is fine during development.
+magenta "?" placeholder, which is fine during development.
 
